public with sharing class LineItemDiscountHandler {
    /**
     * Sets standard discounts on new OpportunityLineItems when Discount is null.
     * Preserves lines that already have a discount specified (including 0).
     */
    public static void applyStandardDiscounts(List<OpportunityLineItem> newLineItems) {
        if (newLineItems == null || newLineItems.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Set<Id> productIds = new Set<Id>();
        Set<Id> pricebookEntryIds = new Set<Id>();

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLineItems) {
            if (line.Discount == null) {
                if (line.Product2Id != null) {
                    productIds.add(line.Product2Id);
                } else if (line.PricebookEntryId != null) {
                    pricebookEntryIds.add(line.PricebookEntryId);
                }
            }
        }

        if (productIds.isEmpty() && pricebookEntryIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Map Product2Id -> Family
        Map<Id, String> familyByProductId = new Map<Id, String>();
        if (!productIds.isEmpty()) {
            for (Product2 prod : [
                SELECT Id, Family 
                FROM Product2 
                WHERE Id IN :productIds
            ]) {
                familyByProductId.put(prod.Id, prod.Family);
            }
        }

        // Map PricebookEntryId -> Family as fallback if Product2Id was not populated directly
        Map<Id, String> familyByPricebookEntryId = new Map<Id, String>();
        if (!pricebookEntryIds.isEmpty()) {
            for (PricebookEntry pbe : [
                SELECT Id, Product2.Family 
                FROM PricebookEntry 
                WHERE Id IN :pricebookEntryIds
            ]) {
                if (pbe.Product2 != null) {
                    familyByPricebookEntryId.put(pbe.Id, pbe.Product2.Family);
                }
            }
        }

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLineItems) {
            if (line.Discount == null) {
                String family = null;
                Boolean hasProduct = false;

                if (line.Product2Id != null) {
                    hasProduct = true;
                    family = familyByProductId.get(line.Product2Id);
                } else if (line.PricebookEntryId != null) {
                    hasProduct = true;
                    family = familyByPricebookEntryId.get(line.PricebookEntryId);
                }

                if (hasProduct) {
                    line.Discount = PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Why use a Map instead of a nested loop?

A nested loop over 3,000 leads and 3,000 contacts requires up to **9,000,000 iterations** in the worst case. In Apex, performing 9 million loop iterations and string comparisons will exceed the synchronous Apex CPU timeout (10,000 ms), causing an uncatchable `System.LimitException: Apex CPU time limit exceeded`.

Instead, using a `Map<String, Id>` reduces the time complexity from **$O(N \times M)$** to **$O(N + M)$**:
1. Iterate over `contacts` once (~3,000 operations) to build a map keyed by normalized email, retaining the first Contact Id seen.
2. Iterate over `leads` once (~3,000 operations) doing $O(1)$ map lookups.

This runs in milliseconds, handles the full dataset safely within standard governor limits, and satisfies all matching, ordering, and blank-handling requirements.

