```apex
public class CaseSurveyJob implements Queueable, Database.AllowsCallouts {
    private Set<Id> caseIds;

    public CaseSurveyJob(Id caseId) {
        this.caseIds = (caseId != null) ? new Set<Id>{ caseId } : new Set<Id>();
    }

    public CaseSurveyJob(Set<Id> caseIds) {
        this.caseIds = (caseIds != null) ? caseIds : new Set<Id>();
    }

    public CaseSurveyJob(List<Id> caseIds) {
        this.caseIds = (caseIds != null) ? new Set<Id>(caseIds) : new Set<Id>();
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        if (this.caseIds == null || this.caseIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<Case> cases = [
            SELECT Id, CaseNumber, ContactId
            FROM Case
            WHERE Id IN :this.caseIds AND ContactId != null
        ];

        List<Task> tasksToInsert = new List<Task>();
        Date dueDate = Date.today().addDays(2);

        for (Case c : cases) {
            tasksToInsert.add(new Task(
                WhatId = c.Id,
                WhoId = c.ContactId,
                Subject = 'CSAT survey: ' + c.CaseNumber,
                ActivityDate = dueDate,
                Status = 'Not Started'
            ));
        }

        if (!tasksToInsert.isEmpty()) {
            // allOrNone = false ensures one task failure does not block the rest
            Database.insert(tasksToInsert, false);
        }
    }
}
```

### Why the Proposed Approach is a Bad Idea

1. **Email Invocation Governor Limit (10 Calls per Transaction)**  
   Apex enforces a strict governor limit of **10 `Messaging.sendEmail` calls per transaction** (`Limits.getLimitEmailInvocations()`). If you call `Messaging.sendEmail` inside the loop for up to 200 opportunities, the transaction will fail on the 11th iteration with an uncatchable `System.LimitException: Too many Email Invocations: 11`, crashing the scheduled job and rolling back execution.

2. **Looping is Unnecessary for Partial Success**  
   The stated motivation—ensuring that if one email fails, the rest still go out—is already natively supported by bulk `Messaging.sendEmail`:
   ```apex
   Messaging.SendEmailResult[] results = Messaging.sendEmail(emails, false);
   ```
   Setting the `allOrNone` parameter (`opt_allOrNone`) to `false` allows Salesforce to deliver all valid messages while reporting failures on invalid ones via the returned `Messaging.SendEmailResult[]` array, all while consuming only **1** email invocation.

